## <a name="xmldoc"></a>5. XML-Documentation comments

One of the useful features of C# is the ability to write documentation directly in the source code using XML-documentation comments. These are used to provide a brief explanation of the purpose of a method, why it's used, and the usage or syntax so that users can understand it better. Here is a basic example of how you would write an XML-documentation comment:

```csharp
    /// <summary>
    /// This method calculates the Square of a number
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="number">The number to be squared.</param>
    /// <returns>The square of the input number.</returns>
    public static double Square(double number)
    {
        return number * number;
    }
```

In the above code, everything enclosed between `///` and `</summary>` is known as summary comments and those enclosed between `<param>` and `</param>` are known as parameter comments. The comments enclosed between `<returns>` and `</returns>` are known as return value comments. This not only helps other programmers who read your code but can also be used by the Visual Studio IDE to display comment snippets in code intellisense.

In XML Doc comments, there are tags for specifying:

- `<summary>`: The summary comment for the type or member provides high-level information about the element.
- `<param>`: The param comment describes the role of a method parameter.
- `<returns>`: The returns comment describes the return value of a method.
- `<value>`: The value comment describes the value that is returned by a property set method.
- `<remarks>`: The remarks comment provides supplemental information about the type or member, such as the constraints on its usage.
- `<example>`: The example comment provides an example of how to use this method or class.
- `<exception>`: The exception comment lists the conditions under which an exception is thrown by a method or property.
- `<see>`: The see comment provides a way for the current member to reference another member, type, or URI in the code.
- `<seealso>`: The seealso comment specifies additional members of the same type, that is, another member that references the current member, such as a related method or property.
  
These comments facilitate readability, maintainability, and understanding of the code, helping developers and documentation engineers to collaborate more effectively. 

Always ensure you're writing clear and concise comments that make logical, concise sentences for readability and maintainability.

